WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:06.920
It's a tough gig being a pollinator
these days, especially in big cities.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.940
Land is being used differently and
that's a threat to pollinating insects.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.800
But a new study of four UK cities
shows that increasing urbanization

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.560
doesn't have to be a death sentence.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.660
A team of ecologists looked at nine
types of land use that make up most

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:29.600
of a city's area, if you exclude buildings,
roads and water which aren't much use

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.900
to pollinators anyway.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.600
First, they counted the total number of
pollinators and plants in each area,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.620
recording how many different 
species there were as well.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:45.060
The more numerous and varied the plants, 
the greater the biodiversity of pollinators.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.060
No surprises there.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:51.240
Gardens held the most and
most varied pollinating insects,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.720
but not all garden plants
are equally appealing.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.520
Hydrangeas, daisies and forget-me-nots
were less appetising, whereas among

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.120
pollinators' favourite flowers were
dandelions, brambles and buttercups -

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.020
ones that gardeners often pull up.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.700
Next, the researchers wanted to know
how land could be better managed

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.920
to support pollinator communities,
so they built a computer model

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:20.740
to test how giving more land
to each different land use

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:24.760
might make pollinator
networks more robust,

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.900
in other words, more
resilient to species loss.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.000
They found that the biggest
bounce in robustness came when

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.880
they allotted more space to allotments,
even though, with the boost,

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.820
allotments still only accounted for
less than 1% of city space.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.300
Allotments host fruits, vegetables
and weeds as well - a mix

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.840
which provides a great variety of flowers.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.700
The researchers also wondered if
there were other ways to boost

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.780
the robustness of pollinator communities.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.260
According to their model, this could be
achieved by planting more flowers in parks

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:05.600
and allowing wildflowers to grow
in verges and other green spaces.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.400
A few simple strategies,
say the researchers,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.280
could help support pollinators
in cities across the world.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.800
Add more allotments,
plant flowers in parks

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.080
and don't mow the grass!

